# deploy/schedule.env.example
# ContentEngineAI scheduled-sweep configuration
#
# Copy this file to deploy/schedule.env and edit what you need:
#
#     cp deploy/schedule.env.example deploy/schedule.env
#
# The copy is gitignored (".gitignore" line 42, "*.env"); this sample is
# committed. Values here shape the systemd unit files, which is why they
# cannot live in config/publisher.yaml: systemd forbids variable expansion in
# the ExecStart executable path, and OnCalendar= sits in a timer unit that
# reads no environment file at all. Both have to be substituted before the
# unit is written.
#
# Legend:
#   [AUTO]     - detected when unset; override only if the guess is wrong
#   [OPTIONAL] - has a default; set it to change behaviour
#
# Re-run `make install-analytics-timer` after editing. systemd does not pick
# up a changed schedule on its own.
#
# Precedence is environment, then this file, then the built-in default, so a
# one-off override needs no edit:
#
#     NOTIFY_ON_FAILURE=0 make install-analytics-timer

# =============================================================================
# PATHS
# =============================================================================

# Repository root the sweep runs from.
#
# [AUTO] Defaults to the parent of this file's directory, so a moved or
# renamed checkout keeps working. Set it only to drive a *different* checkout
# than the one holding this file.
#
# This becomes WorkingDirectory= in the unit, and it is load-bearing: the
# sweep's --outputs-dir defaults to the relative path "outputs", so a wrong
# value writes the figures into some other tree and still reports success.
#REPO_DIR=""

# Python interpreter that runs the sweep.
#
# [AUTO] Defaults to `make print-python`, the same discovery chain the
# *-lowpri targets use, which prefers the interpreter named by .python-version
# because it is the only candidate that names THIS project.
#
# Do not point this at `poetry run python` or at a shim. A user service
# inherits no login shell, so its PATH has no pyenv shims; and poetry.toml
# sets virtualenvs.create = false, which makes `poetry run python` resolve the
# base interpreter rather than the project environment. The unit must name a
# real interpreter absolutely. `poetry env info -p` prints yours.
#PYTHON=""

# =============================================================================
# SCHEDULE
# =============================================================================

# When the sweep runs, in systemd OnCalendar syntax. `systemd-analyze calendar
# "<value>"` shows you the next few firings before you commit to one.
#
# [OPTIONAL] Default: daily (00:00).
#
# Daily is the right default and weekly is not. The provider's per-post
# timeline stops reaching back at roughly five weeks, and the durability ratio
# needs a post past day 30 while still inside that horizon -- a window about
# five days wide. A weekly sweep can step over a post's only window entirely.
ON_CALENDAR="daily"

# Jitter added to each firing, in seconds.
#
# [OPTIONAL] Default: 900 (15 minutes). Keeps the sweep off the exact minute
# every other timer on the machine fires, and off a fixed second at the
# provider.
RANDOMIZED_DELAY_SEC="900"

# How long a single sweep may run before systemd calls it failed.
#
# [OPTIONAL] Default: 30min. Do not set this to "infinity".
#
# systemd disables the start timeout by default for Type=oneshot units. Left
# disabled, a hung HTTP call leaves the unit activating forever; systemd then
# refuses to start a second instance, so every later firing is dropped and the
# unit never reaches "failed" -- which means the failure handler never runs
# either. A stuck sweep would look exactly like a working one.
TIMEOUT_START_SEC="30min"

# =============================================================================
# FAILURE REPORTING
# =============================================================================

# Report a failed sweep. Set to 0 to install no failure handler.
#
# [OPTIONAL] Default: 1.
#
# When on, a failure is written to the journal, appended to
# outputs/logs/analytics-failures.log, and shown as a desktop notification if
# a session is available. The log file is the durable one: a notification
# raised while nobody is at the machine is indistinguishable from none.
NOTIFY_ON_FAILURE="1"
